Tinea Cruris (Jock Itch)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Inguinal erythematous plaques, Sparing of the scrotum/penis, Advancing scaly borders

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-043
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe burning and intractable pruritus in the groin, leading to severe skin breakdown and inability to walk comfortably.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient experiencing an acute, severe burning rash in the bilateral groin that had suddenly worsened, causing extreme pain with friction and resulting in difficulty walking. The patient had been using an inappropriate over-the-counter strong steroid cream, worsening the condition. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for secondary cellulitis, diagnose the fungal exacerbation (tinea incognito), and initiate potent specific antifungal therapy while ceasing the harmful steroid use before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My groin is completely raw and burning. It itches so badly I can't stop scratching, and now it hurts too much to even walk or wear underwear."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-week history of well-demarcated erythematous scaling plaques in the bilateral inguinal folds. The rash features intense pruritus exacerbated by sweating or friction (the patient is a heavy laborer). The lesions exhibit advancing arcuate scaly borders spreading outward down the inner thighs, with central clearing resulting in a reddish-brown hyperpigmentation. There is explicit sparing of the scrotum and penis. Over the last 4 days, the patient applied a potent OTC steroid cream intended for eczema, which caused an explosive exacerbation of the burning, intense redness, and occasional maceration. The severe mechanical pain upon ambulation prompted the urgent call.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently walking with a wide-based gait to avoid friction.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe discomfort and limitation of daily activities due to pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, shifting weight constantly while seated, clearly avoiding bringing the legs together.
Physical Exam: Examination of the bilateral inguinal folds reveals extensive, well-demarcated, highly erythematous plaques extending 10 cm down the medial thighs. The borders are vividly raised, arcuate, and highly scaly (advancing scaly borders). The central areas are hyperpigmented with mild, shiny atrophy (consistent with inappropriate topical steroid use). Crucially, there is complete sparing of the scrotum and penis. There is minor serous maceration deep in the inguinal creases due to occlusion. There are absolutely no satellite papules or pustules (ruling out candidiasis). No spreading induration or fluctuance to suggest bacterial cellulitis.
Environmental Observations: Patient is wearing tight, synthetic athletic underwear.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has been applying a friend's prescription Betamethasone valerate 0.1% cream.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tinea Cruris (Tinea Incognito exacerbation) - Status: Acute, severe symptomatic flare
Differentials:

Candidal Intertrigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct sparing of the scrotum/penis, absence of satellite pustules, and the presence of a clearly demarcated, active scaly advancing border rather than uniform beefy-red erythema.

Erythrasma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The intense, acute pruritus and highly inflammatory raised scaly borders; erythrasma is typically mildly pruritic, flat, and presents with a fine, wrinkled scale.

Inverse Psoriasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The asymmetrical advancing borders with central clearing, and the lack of a smooth, shiny, non-scaly erythematous plaque typical of flexural psoriasis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of Tinea Cruris, dramatically worsened by the inappropriate application of a potent topical corticosteroid (tinea incognito). Triage correctly identified the severe pain with ambulation and skin breakdown as requiring urgent assessment. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was critical to physically examine the groin, definitively distinguish this dermatophyte infection from candidiasis or bacterial cellulitis, and immediately order the cessation of the harmful steroid cream. Prescribing targeted topical and systemic antifungal therapy, along with barrier protection instructions, provided rapid symptom relief and prevented further iatrogenic damage to the skin barrier.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed immediate cessation of all topical corticosteroid creams. Prescribed topical Terbinafine 1% cream applied BD. Given the severity, extent, and previous steroid use, prescribed a short course of oral Terbinafine 250mg daily for 2 weeks to ensure complete eradication. Recommended application of a zinc oxide barrier cream over the treated areas to reduce friction.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly avoiding the strong steroid cream, you will stop feeding the fungus and allow your skin's immune system to recover and fight off the infection. By wearing exclusively loose, cotton boxer shorts, you will drastically reduce the sweat and friction in your groin, instantly relieving the sharp pain when you walk and creating an environment where the fungus cannot survive.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to ensure complete resolution and assess for any permanent skin thinning from the steroid use.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness suddenly becomes hot, swollen, and spreads rapidly, if you develop a fever, or if you notice thick, foul-smelling yellow pus, present to a medical centre or Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Because you used a strong steroid, the rash will look very angry and red for the first 3-4 days after stopping it before the anti-fungal takes full effect.
Clinical Course: The severe burning and itching will significantly reduce within 4-5 days on the oral and topical medication. The dark brown discoloration (hyperpigmentation) will take months to completely fade.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Continue the topical cream for a full 7 days after the skin looks completely clear to prevent an immediate relapse.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have a common fungal infection (jock itch) that was accidentally supercharged by the strong steroid cream, which suppressed your local immune system. We are stopping the steroid and hitting the fungus aggressively with an anti-fungal cream and a tablet to kill it from both the inside and outside.
Actionable Steps: Always completely dry your groin with a separate towel after showering. Treat any concurrent athlete's foot to prevent reinfecting your groin when putting on underwear.

